(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-3-gi-quiz-version-4"]={"title":"GI Physiology — Quiz 3 (30 Questions)","category":"Physiology","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"The GI tract wall is organized, from outer to inner, as serosa, longitudinal muscle, circular muscle, submucosa, and mucosa. The myenteric (Auerbach's) plexus lies specifically...","choices":["outside the serosa, in the mesentery","between the longitudinal and circular muscle layers","within the mucosa itself, beneath the epithelial lining","between the submucosa and the muscularis mucosae"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The myenteric plexus sits between the longitudinal and circular smooth-muscle layers, which is why it primarily controls motility (contraction of those layers). — GI Physiology, Slide 6"},{"q":"Compared to the myenteric plexus, the submucosal (Meissner's) plexus is located in the mucosal/submucosal layer and is primarily concerned with...","choices":["local control of secretion, absorption, and mucosal blood flow","relaying visceral pain sensation directly to the spinal cord","controlling the strength and rhythm of muscle contractions","generating the basic electrical slow-wave rhythm"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Sitting within the mucosal/submucosal layer, the submucosal plexus is positioned to sense the local luminal environment and regulate secretion, absorption, and local blood flow. — GI Physiology, Slide 6"},{"q":"A GI signaling molecule that is released into the blood and acts on a distant target cell is classified as...","choices":["an endocrine (hormone)","an autocrine (self-signal)","a paracrine (local signal)","a neurocrine (synapse)"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. By definition, an endocrine signal is released into the blood to act on a distant target cell; all GI hormones are peptides released this way. — GI Physiology, Slide 9"},{"q":"Somatostatin, which is released by D cells and diffuses locally to inhibit neighboring gastrin-secreting G cells, is best classified as a...","choices":["paracrine","hormone (endocrine)","exocrine","neurocrine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Somatostatin is a peptide paracrine — released by D cells, it diffuses through the interstitium to act on neighboring cells (like G cells) rather than traveling through the blood. — GI Physiology, Slide 9"},{"q":"There are six named sphincters along the alimentary tract between segments. Which list correctly names all six?","choices":["Upper esophageal, lower esophageal, pyloric, ileocecal, internal anal, external anal","Pyloric, ileocecal, internal anal, external anal, sphincter of Oddi, ampullary sphincter","Upper esophageal, lower esophageal, pyloric, ileocecal, internal anal only (five total)","Upper esophageal, lower esophageal, pyloric, cardiac, ileocecal, external anal"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The six sphincters listed are the UES, LES, pyloric sphincter, ileocecal valve/sphincter, and the internal and external anal sphincters. — GI Physiology, Slide 5"},{"q":"The upper esophageal sphincter (UES) and lower esophageal sphincter (LES) serve distinct purposes: the UES mainly...","choices":["keeps air from entering the esophagus","relaxes tonically at rest to allow constant food passage","prevents acid reflux from the stomach into the esophagus","is regulated only by circulating hormones, not by nerves"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The UES's main purpose is to keep air out of the esophagus (e.g., during breathing), while the LES's purpose is to prevent gastric acid reflux. — GI Physiology, Slide 45"},{"q":"During receptive relaxation of the LES and gastric fundus as a meal arrives, the transmitters mediating this relaxation are...","choices":["gastrin and histamine, via an excitatory sympathetic reflex","acetylcholine and substance P, via an excitatory vagovagal reflex","norepinephrine acting on alpha receptors in the enteric plexus","nitric oxide and VIP, via an inhibitory vagovagal reflex"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Receptive relaxation is mediated by a vagovagal reflex in which vagal input is inhibitory, using nitric oxide and VIP as the relaxing transmitters. — GI Physiology, Slides 44, 46"},{"q":"Parasympathetic stimulation of GI smooth muscle is blocked by atropine because it acts through...","choices":["nicotinic receptors on the smooth muscle cell","beta-adrenergic receptors on smooth muscle cells","VIP receptors on smooth muscle cells","muscarinic (M3) receptors on smooth muscle cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Parasympathetic excitation of GI smooth muscle acts on M3 muscarinic receptors, which is exactly the receptor class that atropine (a muscarinic antagonist) blocks. — GI Physiology, Slide 39"},{"q":"Motilin, which drives the interdigestive migrating motor complex, is characteristically released...","choices":["cyclically about every 90 minutes during fasting, and is suppressed by a mixed meal","only in direct response to a high-protein meal reaching the duodenum","continuously at a fixed rate regardless of feeding or fasting state","exclusively by the pancreas in direct response to circulating CCK levels"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Motilin is released in a cyclic pattern roughly every 90 minutes during the fasting state; ingesting a mixed meal suppresses its release, while acid and fat in the duodenum can actually stimulate it. — GI Physiology, Slide 55"},{"q":"Unlike acid (HCl) secretion, gastrin release from G cells is characteristically...","choices":["stimulated only by protein in the duodenum, never the stomach","entirely independent of luminal pH","not blocked by atropine","blocked by atropine, just like acid secretion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A key distinguishing feature noted in these slides is that gastrin secretion is not blocked by atropine, even though direct parietal-cell acid secretion is. — GI Physiology, Slide 90"},{"q":"Besides the indirect somatostatin-mediated pathway, a falling gastric pH also inhibits acid output through a second mechanism, in which acid...","choices":["converts inactive renin into its active form in the stomach","has a direct suppressive effect on parietal cell secretion","is neutralized entirely before it can have any local effect","directly stimulates chief cells to secrete more pepsinogen"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In addition to stimulating somatostatin (which inhibits gastrin), acid has its own direct inhibitory effect on parietal cell secretion, giving two overlapping negative-feedback pathways. — GI Physiology, Slide 94"},{"q":"Histamine released from enterochromaffin-like (ECL) cells is trophic to the oxyntic (acid-secreting) mucosa, meaning it...","choices":["permanently destroys parietal cells after repeated prolonged stimulation over time","converts chief cells into parietal cells after chronic repeated stimulation","stimulates DNA, RNA, and protein synthesis in cells such as parietal and chief cells","has no effect on cells beyond its acute, short-lived acid secretion response"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Beyond its acute secretory effect, histamine has a trophic action on the oxyntic mucosa — stimulating DNA, RNA, and protein synthesis in parietal cells, chief cells, and ECL cells themselves. — GI Physiology, Slide 11"},{"q":"Brunner's glands, found specifically in the duodenum, protect the mucosa from acidic chyme by secreting...","choices":["intrinsic factor for vitamin B12 absorption","bile salts to assist fat emulsification","an alkaline mucus","hydrochloric acid to continue chemical digestion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Brunner's glands are compound mucous glands located in the duodenum that secrete an alkaline mucus, helping neutralize acidic chyme as it leaves the stomach. — GI Physiology, Slides 70, 111"},{"q":"In the wall of the large intestine, the longitudinal smooth muscle is arranged into three distinct bands known as the...","choices":["rugae","plicae circulares","teniae coli","haustra"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Unlike the rest of the GI tract, the colon's longitudinal muscle is concentrated into three longitudinal bands called the teniae coli, rather than forming a continuous layer. — GI Physiology, Slide 61"},{"q":"Haustra, the pouch-like segments of the colon, are notable in that they...","choices":["are formed exclusively by the circular muscle layer, never the longitudinal layer","are permanent, fixed anatomical structures that never change shape","are not fixed structures — they appear and disappear as haustration proceeds","function to rapidly propel stool toward the rectum"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Haustra are dynamic, not permanent structures; they form and dissipate with the colon's slow mixing (haustral) contractions. — GI Physiology, Slide 61"},{"q":"Cholecystokinin (CCK) release from duodenal I-cells is triggered specifically by fatty acids and monoglycerides in the lumen, but notably NOT by...","choices":["intact (undigested) triglycerides","monoglycerides specifically","weak acid in the duodenal lumen","peptides and single amino acids"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. CCK release requires the digestion products of fat — fatty acids or monoglycerides — while intact triglycerides themselves are not an effective stimulus, which is one reason adequate lipase activity is needed to trigger the CCK response. — GI Physiology, Slide 13"},{"q":"CCK inhibits gastric emptying mechanically by...","choices":["directly paralyzing all gastric smooth muscle","contracting the fundus and relaxing the pylorus","relaxing the fundus and constricting the pylorus","stimulating gastrin release, which then slows emptying"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. CCK slows gastric emptying by relaxing the gastric fundus (reducing the pressure gradient pushing chyme out) while constricting the pylorus, together limiting the rate contents leave the stomach. — GI Physiology, Slide 14"},{"q":"I-cells, which release CCK, are distributed mainly in the...","choices":["duodenum and jejunum, with small amounts from the ileum","colon and rectum exclusively, with none from the small intestine","stomach antrum and fundus exclusively, with none from the intestine","esophagus and proximal stomach only, with none more distally"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. CCK-secreting I-cells are concentrated in the duodenum and jejunum, with only small amounts released more distally from the ileum. — GI Physiology, Slide 13"},{"q":"Among the pancreatic carbohydrate-digesting enzymes, pancreatic amylase is notable because it is described as...","choices":["the only luminal enzyme responsible for carbohydrate digestion","one of several redundant luminal enzymes for starch digestion","secreted by the liver rather than the pancreas","active only on dietary proteins, not on starches at all"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pancreatic amylase, which breaks down starches and glycogen into disaccharides, is the only luminal (intraluminal) enzyme for carbohydrate digestion — final disaccharide digestion instead occurs at the brush border, not free in the lumen. — GI Physiology, Slide 101"},{"q":"Among the pancreatic proteolytic enzymes, trypsin and chymotrypsin cleave proteins into polypeptides, while carboxypeptidase's distinct role is to...","choices":["digest starches into disaccharides","emulsify dietary fat for lipase to act on","cleave polypeptides into individual amino acids","convert trypsinogen into active trypsin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Carboxypeptidase acts as an exopeptidase, removing terminal amino acids from polypeptides, complementing trypsin and chymotrypsin's endopeptidase action of cleaving intact proteins into smaller polypeptides. — GI Physiology, Slide 100"},{"q":"Pancreatic lipase, phospholipase, and cholesterol esterase are all pancreatic enzymes for fat digestion. Which pairing of enzyme to its product is correct?","choices":["Pancreatic lipase acts on polypeptides to yield amino acids","Pancreatic lipase acts on fat to yield fatty acids and monoglycerides","Cholesterol esterase acts on starches to yield disaccharides","Phospholipase acts on fat to yield fatty acids and monoglycerides"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Pancreatic lipase hydrolyzes triglyceride (fat) into fatty acids and monoglycerides, while phospholipase acts on phospholipids and cholesterol esterase acts on cholesterol esters, each yielding a fatty acid. — GI Physiology, Slide 102"},{"q":"The pancreas has substantial secretory reserve: enzyme secretion must fall to what fraction of normal before digestive problems occur?","choices":["75–80% of normal","10–15% of normal","Any measurable drop below 100% of normal","50% of normal"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Because all pancreatic enzymes are secreted in excess, digestive problems only emerge once enzyme secretion drops to roughly 10–15% of normal output — a large functional reserve. — GI Physiology, Slide 100"},{"q":"Beyond bile formation and metabolism, the liver's major listed functions also include storage of vitamins and iron and formation of...","choices":["digestive enzymes for protein digestion","coagulation factors","red blood cells throughout adult life","gastric acid for the stomach"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Among its major functions, the liver forms coagulation factors, in addition to filtering/storing blood, metabolizing fats/carbohydrates/proteins, forming bile, and storing vitamins and iron. — Ch 71 Liver, Slide 2"},{"q":"The liver's macrophage system, which phagocytoses bacteria and debris from portal blood, is composed of...","choices":["Kupffer cells","cholangiocytes","hepatocytes","stellate (Ito) cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Kupffer cells are the resident macrophages lining the liver's sinusoids, forming the liver's macrophage system that filters bacteria and particulate matter out of portal blood before it reaches the systemic circulation. — Ch 71 Liver, Slide 3"},{"q":"The liver serves as a blood reservoir, normally holding about what fraction of total blood volume, a volume that can rise substantially in heart failure?","choices":["About 50% of total blood volume","About 10% of total blood volume (roughly 450 mL)","About 25% of total blood volume","Essentially none — the liver holds no significant reservoir of blood"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Normal liver blood volume is about 10% of total blood volume, around 450 mL — a reserve that can increase by as much as 1 L in heart failure, or fall by 30–40% during exercise/hemorrhage via sympathetic stimulation. — Ch 71 Liver, Slide 5"},{"q":"Roughly half of all body lymph is formed in the liver, arising from fluid and protein that leak through porous fenestrations of the sinusoidal endothelium into a space known as the...","choices":["central vein","space of Disse","space of Bowman","portal triad"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Fenestrated sinusoidal endothelial cells allow fluid and plasma proteins to leak into the space of Disse (between endothelium and hepatocytes); this fluid then collects into lymph vessels, accounting for about half of all body lymph. — Ch 71 Liver, Slide 6"},{"q":"Within the pyloric gland area of the stomach, the two main cell types are G-cells (which release gastrin) and...","choices":["chief cells, which secrete large amounts of pepsinogen for digestion","mucus neck cells, which secrete mucus and small amounts of pepsinogen","parietal cells, which secrete hydrochloric acid into the lumen","I-cells, which release cholecystokinin into the bloodstream"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The pyloric gland is composed mainly of gastrin-releasing G-cells and mucus neck cells (secreting mucus and a small amount of pepsinogen) — a different cell composition than the oxyntic (body) glands, which instead feature parietal and chief cells. — GI Physiology, Slide 80"},{"q":"Gastric acid (HCl) serves three major physiological functions: it is bacteriostatic, converts pepsinogen to pepsin, and also...","choices":["digests dietary starch directly via gastric amylase activity","neutralizes duodenal bicarbonate secreted by the pancreas","helps begin protein digestion (working with pepsin)","emulsifies dietary fat through direct detergent-like action"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Gastric acid's three listed major functions are: bacteriostatic action, converting pepsinogen to pepsin, and helping begin protein digestion together with the resulting pepsin. — GI Physiology, Slide 82"},{"q":"Similar to trypsin's autocatalytic activation of trypsinogen, once some pepsin is formed from pepsinogen by acid, pepsin itself then...","choices":["is immediately destroyed by the acid that activated it","converts more pepsinogen into pepsin","stops all further pepsinogen secretion via negative feedback","converts trypsinogen to trypsin in the stomach"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. After acid initiates the conversion of pepsinogen to pepsin, pepsin then autocatalytically converts additional pepsinogen into pepsin, amplifying active enzyme formation — directly analogous to trypsin's autocatalytic activation of trypsinogen in the intestine. — GI Physiology, Slide 83"},{"q":"Of the roughly 6700 mL of total daily GI secretions (saliva, gastric, pancreatic, bile, small intestinal, Brunner's gland, and large intestinal secretions combined), the single largest contributor by volume is...","choices":["saliva, at about 1000 mL/day from the salivary glands","small intestinal secretion, at about 1800 mL/day","bile, at about 1000 mL/day produced by the liver","gastric secretion, at about 1500 mL/day"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Small intestinal secretion contributes the largest share, about 1800 mL/day, edging out gastric secretion (about 1500 mL/day), with saliva, pancreatic secretion, and bile each contributing roughly 1000 mL/day. — GI Physiology, Slide 70"}]};
